/// Site capture — the photos (and later video) a crew takes on a job.
///
/// ONE TABLE, HUNG OFF `Job`. The same call `ComplianceDocument` makes for
/// its four document types, for the same reason: a photo of a cracked
/// header, a photo proving a punch item was fixed and a photo attached to
/// a safety incident are the same shape of record — a file, a job, a
/// person, a time — differing only in what they are later attached to.
/// Four near-identical tables would each need their own upload path, their
/// own gallery and their own delete guard.
///
/// `jobId` IS REQUIRED, and that is the design. Every other thing this
/// feature will eventually attach to — a punch item, a safety incident, a
/// daily report — already belongs to exactly one job, so the job is the
/// spine and never has to be inferred. It also means the GC question this
/// feature exists to answer ("show me everything from the west wall in
/// August") is one indexed query, not a union across attachment types.
///
/// DELIBERATELY NO ATTACHMENT COLUMNS YET. The obvious next move is a
/// nullable `punchListItemId`, `safetyIncidentId`, `dailyFieldReportId`
/// and so on. Each one arrives with the code that writes and reads it, not
/// before: this repo has a standing scar from columns that were added,
/// documented and never selected by anything (`acknowledgedSeverity`), and
/// a nullable FK nothing populates looks identical to one whose feature is
/// broken.
///
/// NOT AN EVIDENCE RECORD IN THE `Submittal`/`Rfi` SENSE — no counter, no
/// sequence number, no send-then-lock lifecycle. A photo is not
/// correspondence and nobody transmits "photo 14" to a GC. What it does
/// borrow from that family is the locked identity: see `capturedAt` below.
model JobMedia {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  companyId String
  company   Company @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])
  jobId     String
  job       Job     @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id])

  /// Where the file actually lives, and the URL that is rendered. Vercel
  /// Blob, `access: "public"` with a random suffix — unguessable rather
  /// than private, matching every other upload in this app. See
  /// apps/web/lib/blob.ts for why that distinction is written down
  /// everywhere it applies.
  ///
  /// IT IS ALSO WHAT THE FILE IS DELETED BY, and the reason there is no
  /// `blobPathname` column beside it. There was one, whose own comment
  /// said it existed so deletion was possible — and `deleteJobMedia`
  /// deleted by `blobUrl`, so nothing ever read it. `del` takes "Blob url
  /// (or pathname)" (@vercel/blob@2.8.0 dist/index.d.ts:75), so the URL
  /// was always sufficient, and a second column holding what this one's
  /// own path already spells is a second source of truth for the same
  /// string.
  ///
  /// The path in this URL is not decoration either: it is
  /// `job-media/<jobId>/<file>`, and `recordJobMedia` refuses to write a
  /// row whose URL sits anywhere else. That prefix is what makes this
  /// column safe to hand to `del()` — see apps/web/lib/job-media.ts.
  blobUrl String

  /// What the browser said it uploaded, and how big it was.
  ///
  /// `contentType` IS BRANCHED ON, and this comment said the opposite until
  /// 2026-09-11. It read: "only photos can be uploaded, so the gallery
  /// renders an image unconditionally", and that the `jobMediaKind()` helper
  /// which mapped this column to a kind "is gone" for want of a caller. Both
  /// sentences were true when written and stopped being true on 2026-09-10,
  /// when #230 shipped video and voice notes: `jobMediaKind()` is back in
  /// apps/web/lib/job-media.ts, it has five callers (the token route, the
  /// recording action, both galleries and the portal projection), and every
  /// surface renders an `<img>`, a `<video>` or an `<audio>` from what it
  /// returns.
  ///
  /// What has NOT changed is the reason the column is the only stored fact
  /// here: the kind is DERIVED from it on every read and stored nowhere,
  /// because a `kind` column beside the content type is a second source of
  /// truth that can disagree with it. The helper coming back is what made
  /// that rule load-bearing rather than theoretical.
  contentType String
  byteSize    Int

  /// What the person typed about the photo. Optional — a photo with no
  /// caption is still worth having, and demanding one gets you "photo".
  caption String?

  /// When the picture was taken, per the DEVICE. Entered rather than
  /// stamped, the same rule the field reports and submittals follow: a
  /// crew uploading Friday's photos on Monday must not have them recorded
  /// as Monday's.
  ///
  /// A full timestamp, NOT the UTC-midnight date this schema uses
  /// elsewhere, and the deviation is deliberate — on a jobsite the hour
  /// matters ("before the pour", "after the inspector left") and rounding
  /// it to a calendar day throws away the ordering within a day, which is
  /// most of what a walkthrough is.
  capturedAt DateTime

  /// When the server received it. Kept ALONGSIDE `capturedAt` rather than
  /// instead of it because a device clock is set by its owner and is
  /// routinely wrong — a phone fresh off a charger in a trailer with no
  /// signal can be days out. When the two disagree materially the UI can
  /// say so; with only one of them stored, it could not.
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  capturedByUserId String?
  capturedBy       User?   @relation("JobMediaCapturedBy", fields: [capturedByUserId], references: [id])

  /// WHERE THE PHONE WAS WHEN THE FILE WAS CAPTURED, and null — the value
  /// on almost every row — when nobody knows.
  ///
  /// NULL IS AN ORDINARY ROW, NOT A DEGRADED ONE, and that has to be true
  /// before anything else here makes sense. Every capture taken before
  /// 2026-09-11 has no location and none can ever be recovered. A desktop
  /// upload has none. A crew that taps "Don't allow" has none, and that is
  /// a decision the product respects rather than nags about. So nothing
  /// anywhere may treat a missing coordinate as a problem to be fixed: the
  /// card simply does not draw the line, and the gallery's filter offers
  /// "No location" as a first-class half rather than as an exception list.
  ///
  /// THREE COLUMNS, NOT ONE POINT TYPE. PostGIS is not installed and Prisma
  /// cannot express `point` anyway; two floats and a radius are what the
  /// Geolocation API hands over and what every reader here needs. Nothing
  /// in this app does geometry — see the read-time note below on why it
  /// deliberately does not.
  ///
  /// FIVE DECIMAL PLACES, ROUNDED ON THE WAY IN. That is about 1.1 m at the
  /// equator, and it is a ceiling nothing real reaches: a phone's own best
  /// fix is 3-5 m in the open and tens of metres inside a steel building.
  /// A raw fix arrives with 7 decimals — centimetres — which is a precision
  /// the measurement does not have and which reads to anyone looking at it
  /// as though it does. Rounding is done ONCE, by the pure module both
  /// writers share (apps/web/lib/job-media-location.ts), so the stored value
  /// is the displayed value and there is no second rounding at read time to
  /// disagree with the first.
  ///
  /// It is also the only privacy hedge that survives storage: this is not
  /// the job's address, it is where a named crew member was standing, to
  /// the metre, at a stated minute.
  ///
  /// ONLY WHEN THE FIX IS CONTEMPORARY WITH THE CAPTURE. The browser reads
  /// the position at UPLOAD time, and `capturedAt` can be days earlier — a
  /// crew uploading Friday's photos from the office on Monday would
  /// otherwise have every one of them located in the office car park, which
  /// is worse than no location at all because it is confidently wrong. The
  /// client attaches a position only when the file is fresh enough for
  /// "where the phone is now" and "where this was taken" to be the same
  /// sentence (`JOB_MEDIA_LOCATION_FRESHNESS_MS`), and says on screen when
  /// it did not. That rule is CLIENT-SIDE and therefore not a guarantee —
  /// stated plainly rather than implied, because the database cannot check
  /// it: nothing records when the fix was taken, and adding a fourth column
  /// for that would be storing the app's own upload time twice.
  capturedLatitude  Float?
  capturedLongitude Float?

  /// THE ERROR BAR, in metres, and it is stored for the same reason
  /// `createdAt` is stored beside `capturedAt`: without it, two completely
  /// different facts look identical once written down.
  ///
  /// `GeolocationCoordinates.accuracy` is a 95%-confidence radius. A GPS
  /// fix outdoors reports 5; a fix derived from the network or from an IP
  /// address reports 2000 or more, and the second one means "somewhere in
  /// this town" while looking exactly like the first. A coordinate with no
  /// accuracy figure is a claim with no error bar, and this app would then
  /// be printing a 5-decimal number next to a photo on the strength of a
  /// guess about which city the phone was in.
  ///
  /// NOT ROUNDED and not bucketed: it is already an estimate, and rounding
  /// an estimate is how "±2000 m" becomes "±2 km" becomes "roughly here".
  /// Whether a fix is too coarse to mean anything is DERIVED at read time
  /// from this number, never stored — see `job-media-location.ts`.
  ///
  /// Null exactly when the coordinates are null; the database enforces the
  /// pairing, since neither of the two writers can be trusted to remember
  /// it forever (`JobMedia_captured_location_pairing`).
  capturedAccuracyMeters Float?

  /// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT STORED, listed because each one is the
  /// obvious next commit and each one is derived state (CLAUDE.md):
  /// distance from the job's address, an on-site/off-site flag, a
  /// reverse-geocoded street. All three change the moment the job's address
  /// is corrected, and a stored answer would not. There is also no
  /// reverse-geocoding here at all — it would mean sending a crew member's
  /// position to a third party, which this app does not do for anything.
  ///
  /// AND NO INDEX. The only query is `/photos`'s "has a location / has
  /// none", which runs inside a company already narrowed and ordered by
  /// `[companyId, capturedAt]`. A null test on a column that is set on
  /// roughly half of a company's rows has no selectivity worth an index
  /// write on every insert.

  /// WHEN THIS PHOTO WAS SHOWN TO THE CLIENT, and null — the default —
  /// when it never has been. This is the whole of what the GC can see.
  ///
  /// SHARING IS OPT-IN, ONE PHOTO AT A TIME, and that is the important
  /// decision in this feature rather than an incidental one. A job's
  /// photos are not a set a sub wants published: the same gallery holds
  /// the shot of another trade's damage being kept for a backcharge, the
  /// unsafe condition being documented defensively, and the crew's own
  /// mistake before it was put right. `/portal` already withholds job
  /// costing from the client for the same reason — see ARCHITECTURE.md —
  /// and this file's product exists for the sub, not the GC.
  ///
  /// A TIMESTAMP RATHER THAN A BOOLEAN, for the cost of nothing. Showing a
  /// photo to the GC is a disclosure, and in an argument about what they
  /// were told and when, "shared" answers less than "shared on the 4th".
  /// Nullable means never shared, so there is no backfill and no row that
  /// starts life visible.
  ///
  /// NOT DERIVED, so storing it breaks no rule: nothing else in the
  /// database implies this. It is a decision somebody made, which is the
  /// same reason `ComplianceDocument.status` is stored while its
  /// expiry-derived state is not.
  sharedWithClientAt DateTime?

  /// Who shared it. SET NULL on user delete, like `capturedBy` — the fact
  /// of the disclosure outlives the account that made it, and losing the
  /// name is better than losing the record that it happened.
  sharedWithClientByUserId String?
  sharedWithClientBy       User?   @relation("JobMediaSharedBy", fields: [sharedWithClientByUserId], references: [id])

  /// The labels somebody put on this photo. See media-tags.prisma for why
  /// a join to a vocabulary table rather than a string column here.
  tags JobMediaTagAssignment[]

  /// What somebody drew on it. See media-annotations.prisma for why the
  /// marks are rows beside the file rather than pixels burned into it.
  annotations JobMediaAnnotation[]

  /// The two galleries, and one index each. Both order by `capturedAt`
  /// descending, so the sort column belongs in the index rather than in a
  /// sort the database does afterwards.
  ///
  /// `[companyId]` alone used to be the other one, and it could not serve
  /// `/photos` — the company-wide read filters on `companyId` and orders
  /// by `capturedAt`, so every row for the company was read and sorted.
  /// `[companyId, capturedAt]` answers that AND everything the bare
  /// `[companyId]` answered, being its leftmost prefix, so keeping both
  /// would only be a second index to write on every insert.
  @@index([companyId, capturedAt])
  /// The job page's own query: one job, newest first.
  @@index([jobId, capturedAt])
}
